+#### Managed application integration (Unix)
+
+`downloads managed` provides atomic account-scoped playlist snapshots for a local
+application. Use a separate config/cache and authenticate once before starting
+background work; synchronization refreshes cached tokens and never opens a browser.
+
+```sh
+spotify_player -c /sd/offline/config -C /sd/offline/cache downloads managed \
+  --store /sd/offline/store --catalog /run/offline/catalog.json \
+  --job /run/offline/job.json --quota-bytes 0 --request 1000-1 \
+  sync 'spotify:playlist:PLAYLIST_ID'
+```
+
+Actions are `catalog`, `sync URI`, `remove URI`, `forget`, `metadata-login`,
+`login`, `verify-login`, `preview URI --output FILE --offset 0`, and
+`play URI --session 1000-2 --control FILE --status FILE --index 0 [--resume]`.
+The catalog exposes ordered songs, completed snapshots and storage usage. Download
+status includes completed/total tracks and the caller's request identifier. Only
+fully verified generations replace the current snapshot; a failed or interrupted
+update leaves the previous version usable. Unsupported, unavailable or local
+playlist items fail the whole synchronization rather than silently dropping songs.
+
+Playback needs only local files and cached account identity. Its JSON command
+queue accepts numbered pause/resume/next/prev/seek/skip/track/stop actions; status
+acknowledges processed IDs. See [the file protocol](docs/offline.md#managed-file-protocol).
+The store and its temporary files must be on persistent storage. Public status
+contains no audio keys; the private store must remain owner-only. This remains
+experimental and requires Premium for synchronization.
+
